        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 916,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Finds the k largest values of an array - or the k smallest - and gives their positions and the values themselves, best first.\n\nUse it to pick the three most active joints in a frame, the five loudest frequency bands, the nearest few points to a target (k smallest distances), the strongest matches from np.correlate. It works along an axis, so each row of a 2-D array gets its own top k.\n\nThis is not a NumPy function by this name: it is built on np.argpartition, which finds the k without sorting everything else, and then sorts only those k.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.top_k <k: int> <largest or smallest>\nDefaults: 3, largest.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnp.top_k 5\nnp.top_k 1 smallest\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is searched and sent.\n\nk (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many to find. More than there are gives all of them, sorted.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to look: -1 (the default) within each row, 0 down each column.\n\nwhich:\nAn option, under the node: largest (the default) or smallest.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nindices:\nThe positions of the k values, best first.\n\nvalues:\nThe k values themselves, best first. Sent first, then the indices.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: of 5 9 1 7 3 the two largest are 9 (at position 1) and 7 (at position 3).\n\nBottom: the same list, the two smallest: 1 (at 2) and 3 (at 4). A metro re-sends the list twice a second; edit it and click.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.argmax and np.amax find just the one largest. np.argsort and np.sort order everything. np.take and np.take_along_axis fetch values by position.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Finds the k largest values of an array - or the k smallest - and gives their positions and the values themselves, best first.\n\nUse it to pick the three most active joints in a frame, the five loudest frequency bands, the nearest few points to a target (k smallest distances), the strongest matches from np.correlate. It works along an axis, so each row of a 2-D array gets its own top k.\n\nThis is not a NumPy function by this name: it is built on np.argpartition, which finds the k without sorting everything else, and then sorts only those k.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.top_k <k: int> <largest or smallest>\nDefaults: 3, largest.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnp.top_k 5\nnp.top_k 1 smallest\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is searched and sent.\n\nk (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many to find. More than there are gives all of them, sorted.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to look: -1 (the default) within each row, 0 down each column.\n\nwhich:\nAn option, under the node: largest (the default) or smallest.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nindices:\nThe positions of the k values, best first.\n\nvalues:\nThe k values themselves, best first. Sent first, then the indices.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: of 5 9 1 7 3 the two largest are 9 (at position 1) and 7 (at position 3).\n\nBottom: the same list, the two smallest: 1 (at 2) and 3 (at 4). A metro re-sends the list twice a second; edit it and click.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.argmax and np.amax find just the one largest. np.argsort and np.sort order everything. np.take and np.take_along_axis fetch values by position."
        },
